WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.970
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.210
SARA ROBINSON: Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.050
Welcome to Live Coding,
a machine learning model

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.160
from scratch.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.880
My name is Sara Robinson.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.300
I'm a developer advocate
on the Cloud Platform Team,

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.200
focused on machine learning.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.530
You can find me on
Twitter @SRobTweets.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:24.210
And most recently, you can find
my blog at SaraRobinson.dev.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:27.790
So let's dive right in with
what we're going to cover today.

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:30.270
I'm going to start out with
a quick overview of what

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.430
machine learning is.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.280
Then I'll talk about the
model we'll be building.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.670
And finally, we'll get
to the live coding.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:42.140
So at a high level, what
is machine learning?

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:45.640
I really like this definition--
using data to answer questions.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.980
The idea here is that as we
provide more and more data

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.900
to our machine learning
systems, they'll

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.660
be able to improve and
generalize on examples

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:55.588
that they haven't seen before.

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:57.630
So we can think of almost
any supervised learning

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.040
problem in this way.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.230
We have our label
training inputs.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:05.590
We feed them into our model, and
our model outputs a prediction.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:08.770
Now, these training inputs
could really be anything.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:10.560
It could be the text
of a movie review,

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.440
and our model could be doing
sentiment analysis to tell us

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.180
that this is a positive review.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.510
It could be numerical or
categorical fitness data,

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.970
and maybe our model predicts
the quality of the sleep

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.260
that we'll get.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:24.500
It could be image data.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.440
So in this example,
our model is predicting

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.720
that this is an image of a cat.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.360
So this model concept
can seem really magical,

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.200
but it's actually not magic.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.700
Under the hood, it all boils
down to matrix multiplication.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.640
So if any of you
remember y equals

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.290
mx plus b from your
algebra classes,

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.120
this might look familiar.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.972
The idea here is that you have
your features as matrices.

00:01:49.972 --> 00:01:51.430
Those are your
inputs, and then you

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:53.490
have the thing you're
trying to predict.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:56.225
And then you have these
weight and bias matrices.

00:01:56.225 --> 00:01:57.850
And when these are
initialized, they're

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.500
initialized to random values.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.310
So the idea here is that
as you train your model,

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:06.160
you'll find the optimal values
for these weight and bias

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.222
matrices, so that you get
high-accuracy predictions.

00:02:10.222 --> 00:02:11.680
So that sounds
great, but you might

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.055
be thinking the
examples I showed

00:02:13.055 --> 00:02:16.720
on the previous slides-- none
of those had matrices anywhere.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.780
Well, it turns out that you
can represent pretty much

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:23.100
any type of data as a matrix.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.040
So this image, as an example.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.080
All an image really is is
just a bunch of pixels.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.420
And this is a color image.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:33.500
So each pixel has a red, green,
and blue value, or an RGB

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:34.400
value.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.990
So this image becomes three
matrices of the RGB values

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.140
for each pixel.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:43.920
Let's say you have
categorical data.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.250
So for a particular
column in your data set,

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:48.770
it describes the industry
that a person works in.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.110
And there's three
possible values.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.720
The way you encode categorical
data is through what's

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.490
called one hot encoding.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:58.340
So to do this, you
create an array

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.260
with the number of
elements corresponding

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:03.590
to the number of categories
you have-- in this case, 3.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:07.430
And all of the values are
0, except for a single 1.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:10.490
And the index of that 1
corresponds with the value

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.310
of that category.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:13.910
So maybe you're
thinking, wouldn't it

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:16.610
be easier if we just numerically
encoded these Healthcare

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.340
1, Finance 2, Retail 3?

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.920
We could do that,
but then our model

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.340
would interpret these as
continuous numerical values.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:28.207
So it would assign
higher weight to retail.

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:30.290
It would say retail is
greater than finance, which

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:31.470
is greater than health care.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.050
And we want our model to treat
all these inputs equally,

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:38.000
which is why we one
hot encode them.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.950
So I've shown you
how we can transform

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:41.967
our data for our
machine learning models.

00:03:41.967 --> 00:03:43.550
In this talk, I want
to share with you

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:48.590
some tools on Google Cloud that
can help you build ML models.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:51.920
And I like to visualize this as
a pyramid, where you can choose

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.760
your level of abstraction.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.910
If you want to get down
to the details of matrix

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:57.890
multiplication and
building all the layers

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:00.750
for your model from scratch,
which we're going to do today,

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:01.860
you can do that.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:05.850
But you also don't need ML
expertise to get started.

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.820
So the tools on the
bottom of the pyramid

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:11.660
are targeted more towards data
scientists and ML engineers.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.640
And as we move
towards the top, these

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:17.060
are targeted towards application
developers that may not have

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.908
machine learning expertise.

00:04:19.908 --> 00:04:21.700
So in this talk, we're
going to be building

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:23.410
a model with
TensorFlow, and we're

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:26.550
going to deploy it
on Cloud AI Platform.

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:30.460
The idea here is to turn you
all into data scientists and ML

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.270
engineers.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.440
So when I was thinking
about the type of model

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.180
I wanted to build
for this, I wanted

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.700
to choose something that would
resonate with developers.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.770
And as a developer,
there's one tool

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:44.570
I can think of that I
use on a daily basis,

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:46.540
and that is Stack Overflow.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:49.390
So I wanted to build a
text classification model

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:52.270
to see if we could predict
the tags of a Stack Overflow

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:53.620
question.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.770
To do this, we
need a lot of data.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:59.140
And luckily, we have a
public data set available

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:02.860
in Google Cloud BigQuery,
which is our big data analytics

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:05.100
warehouse on Google
Cloud Platform.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.410
BigQuery has lots of really
interesting public data

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:09.940
sets for you to
explore and play with.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.240
And they happen to have one
of Stack Overflow questions,

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:15.640
and it doesn't have just a
few Stack Overflow questions.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:20.170
It has over 26 gigabytes
and over 17 million rows

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:21.760
of questions.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:24.530
So this is a great
place to get started.

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:26.420
Since we don't
have a ton of time,

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:28.210
I wanted to simplify
the problem space.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:29.710
So in this example,
we're just going

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:33.470
to be classifying questions that
have these five tags related

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:36.150
to data science and
machine learning.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.840
So for this particular
question, our model

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:40.790
should categorize
it as Pandas, which

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:45.200
is a Python library
for data science.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.920
So our first step is to
get the data from BigQuery.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.980
BigQuery has a great web UI,
where we can write SQL directly

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:54.070
in the browser, get our data,
and then download it as a CSV,

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.610
which is what I did.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:59.290
So I'm extracting the title
and the body of the question,

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:01.330
concatenating them
into one field,

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:04.600
getting a comma-separated
string of the tags.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:09.730
And then we're only getting
questions with these five tags.

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:13.330
So I ran this query, and as
I was looking at the results,

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:15.700
I noticed something.

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:17.710
A lot of the
questions, the question

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:21.280
posters conveniently will put
the name of whatever framework

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.690
they're using in the question,
which is very helpful.

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:25.720
But that got me
thinking, do we actually

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:27.760
need machine learning for this?

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.090
Could we just replace our entire
ML system with an if statement?

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.150
If TensorFlow is
in the question,

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.170
tag equals TensorFlow.

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:35.950
The answer is no.

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:39.070
Because although lots of
question posters do this,

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:42.040
there are a lot of people that
post questions that are really

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:45.010
good questions, but they just
dive right into the code.

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.830
And they may not mention
the framework or tag name.

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:50.700
So we want to capture
these questions, too.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.320
And we don't want our model to
just pick up on those signal

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:55.960
words so that it only
identifies questions

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:59.530
with the word TensorFlow
in them as TensorFlow.

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:02.770
We wanted to be able to
generalize to find patterns

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:04.510
among these tags.

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:06.490
So when I was
pre-processing the training

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:08.470
data, what I wanted
to do was take out

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:11.320
these obvious keywords.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:14.230
So I thought I could replace
them with a common word.

00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:17.650
And because everybody
loves avocados,

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.870
I replaced all of
these words, including

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:22.000
abbreviations for
the frameworks,

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.920
with the word avocado.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:28.670
So the results in BigQuery
look something like this.

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:29.680
We have our questions.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.320
We have avocado
predictive models.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:34.840
We have avocado data sets.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:38.933
Lots of avocados everywhere.

00:07:38.933 --> 00:07:40.350
So what I didn't
talk about before

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:44.767
was how we encode freeform
text data into matrices.

00:07:44.767 --> 00:07:47.100
There's a couple of different
approaches for doing this.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:49.140
I'm going to use one
called bag of words.

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:51.820
This is a really simple
one for getting started.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:54.360
So you can think of
each input to your model

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:57.390
in bag of words as a
bag of Scrabble tiles,

00:07:57.390 --> 00:07:59.850
whereas instead of a
letter on each tile,

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:02.010
you have a word on each tile.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:04.950
So this type of model cannot
detect the order of words

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.220
in a sentence, but it can
detect the presence or absence

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.030
of certain words.

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:12.210
So to show you how this
works, I want to show you

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:14.250
a really simple example.

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:17.400
So for this example, we'll limit
our problem space even further

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.350
to say we're only
tagging questions

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:21.030
with three types of tags.

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:25.410
And bag of word models have
a concept called vocabulary.

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:27.750
So pretend for a
moment that you're

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:29.630
learning English
for the first time,

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:31.196
and you only know
these 10 words.

00:08:31.196 --> 00:08:33.404
That's how our model is
going to look at the problem.

00:08:33.404 --> 00:08:36.380
It might lead to some
interesting conversations,

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.320
but pretend we only
know these 10 words.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:41.730
So when we take
this input question,

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:43.860
how to plot data
frame bar graph,

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.710
we'll look at our vocabulary,
and we'll say, OK.

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:48.570
I recognize these three words.

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:50.250
The rest of the
words in the question

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:53.120
are going to be
gibberish to the model.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.930
And when we feed all of our
questions into our model,

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:59.200
we want to feed
them in as matrices

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:00.740
that are the same size.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:04.150
So what we do is our
question becomes an array

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:06.880
the size of our vocabulary,
with ones and zeros

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:10.900
indicating which words from
our vocabulary are present.

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:14.600
So because dataframe is the
first index in our vocabulary,

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.170
the first element of
our array becomes a 1,

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:20.290
even though dataframe is not
the first word in our question.

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:24.010
Same goes for graph and plot.

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:26.920
So to summarize,
our question becomes

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:29.410
a vocabulary-sized
array of 1s and 0s.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:31.720
It's called multi hot encoding.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:34.540
And our prediction-- since
for this particular model,

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:37.660
our model's going to be able
to identify a question that has

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:39.430
multiple tags, not just one.

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:42.470
So this will also be
a multi hot array.

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:47.600
So now we know how to encode
our text data for our model.

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:50.030
What does our model
actually look like?

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:51.460
So the input data
to our model is

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:54.100
going to be that
vocabulary-sized bag of words

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:56.170
matrix.

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:59.415
And then we feed that into
what's called hidden layer.

00:09:59.415 --> 00:10:01.540
So this is going to be a
deep neural network, which

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:04.180
means that we have layers
in between our input

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:05.510
and our output layer.

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:08.080
So it's going to take this
vocabulary-sized array

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.120
and resize it to
whatever size we

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:13.150
choose for our second
layer and our third layer.

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:15.640
Now, the output of
these hidden layers

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:17.170
doesn't mean too much to us.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:18.940
Our model's using
it to represent

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:21.850
complex relationships, but
what we really care about

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:24.250
is the output of
our final layer.

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:26.110
There's many options
for how you can compute

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:27.670
the output of this layer.

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:29.590
We're going to choose
one called sigmoid.

00:10:29.590 --> 00:10:33.970
And what this will do is it will
return a value between 0 and 1

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:37.280
for each tag, which
corresponds to the probability

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:40.880
that that tag is associated
with this question.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:42.760
So for this
particular example, it

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:45.400
looks like the question has
a high probability of being

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:49.470
about Keras or TensorFlow.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:52.440
So what are all the tools we're
going to use to build this?

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:55.530
I already showed you how we
use BigQuery to gather our data

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:57.840
and downloaded the CSV.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:00.060
We're going to use three
open source frameworks

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:02.757
to do some pre-processing
and some transformations

00:11:02.757 --> 00:11:04.590
on our data to get it
into the right format.

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:09.393
We'll use Pandas,
Scikit-learn, and Keras.

00:11:09.393 --> 00:11:11.060
We'll use TensorFlow
to build our model,

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:14.050
specifically tf.keras.

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:17.070
We will run our training
and evaluation in Colab.

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:20.860
Colab is a Python notebook
that's cloud-hosted,

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:22.570
and you can run
it in the browser.

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:26.000
It's totally free
for anyone to use.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:31.010
And finally, we'll deploy our
model on Cloud AI Platform.

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:32.650
So the title says Live Coding.

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:36.000
Let's go over to the demo.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:37.690
Can we switch to the demo?

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:38.830
Awesome.

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:41.200
So here we have
our Colab notebook.

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:42.320
It's connected.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:44.370
Again, anybody can access
Colab in the browser.

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:46.720
Colab.research.google.com.

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:49.390
And as you can see, we've
got no code in here.

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:50.890
What could go wrong?

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:52.120
Just a bunch of comments.

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:55.990
So I'm going to start
writing some code

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:58.910
and run these cell by cell.

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:00.700
So what I'm going
to use is Colab

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:02.980
has this handy snippets tool.

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:06.550
So I've saved a couple of the
snippets for this notebook

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:09.230
that I'm just going
to drop in here.

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:14.050
So the first one is we are
going to run this anyway.

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:17.872
I had to reset the runtime,
and now we are connected.

00:12:17.872 --> 00:12:19.330
So our first cell
is just importing

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:20.997
all of the libraries
we're going to use.

00:12:20.997 --> 00:12:23.470
We're using TensorFlow,
Pandas, NumPy,

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:26.020
a couple of Scikit-learn
utility functions,

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:29.180
and then we're using
Keras to build our model.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:30.690
So we've got all of our imports.

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:32.590
Let me actually make
that just a tad bigger,

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:34.540
so that you can all see.

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:39.190
The next thing we want
to do is authenticate.

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:40.100
Here we go.

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:43.540
So Colab also has a handy
authentication function

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:44.860
that we can run.

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:49.270
And what this will do is
it will pop up a URL for us

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:52.450
to authenticate to
our Cloud account.

00:12:52.450 --> 00:13:02.210
So I will allow access, copy
this code, and paste it in.

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:05.070
So now, we are authenticated.

00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:07.380
And now, we're going to
get to the fun stuff.

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:09.200
So first, we want
to download our CSV.

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:11.480
So now that I'm
authenticated, I have

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:17.150
saved all this data in a
CSV in Google Cloud Storage.

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:20.480
That's our object storage
tool on Google Cloud Platform.

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:24.890
So I'm going to download the
CSV to my local Colab instance.

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:27.950
And then what I'm
going to do is I'm

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:33.500
going to use Pandas
to read this CSV in.

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:37.790
So let me just read it in, here.

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:40.280
And this will transform
our data into what's

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:44.310
called a Pandas Dataframe,
which you'll see in a moment.

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:45.980
The next thing
we're going to do is

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:47.480
we're going to shuffle our data.

00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:53.270
And this is a really important
concept in machine learning.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:55.020
In case your data was
in any sort of order

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:58.110
before, you want to make
sure that you shuffle it.

00:13:58.110 --> 00:14:01.740
And I'm using the Scikit-learn
shuffle function to do that.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:06.210
Data.head will let
us preview our data.

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:07.710
So this is what our
data looks like.

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:10.940
We've got our tags as
comma separated strings,

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:14.670
and we've got our question
text with lots of avocados.

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:17.830
Now, we can't feed this in
its current form to our model.

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:20.160
So we're going to need
to do some encoding.

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:22.860
So first, we'll take
care of the tags.

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:26.760
So the first thing we want
to do is encode these tags,

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:32.300
as we saw on the slides, to
multi hot five element arrays,

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:34.430
because we have
five possible tags.

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:36.110
So what I'm doing
in the first line

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:40.740
is I'm splitting each tag
into an array of strings.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:43.430
And then I'm using this
Scikit-learn multi label

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:46.130
binarizer function,
which will take

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:49.670
all of those arrays of
strings and transform them

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:51.110
into a multi hot array.

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:52.770
So this is for the
first question.

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:55.880
We can see it's about
TensorFlow and Keras.

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:59.610
And here's this reference
array that Scikit-learn

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:01.040
has created for us.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:03.540
So our input becomes this.

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:07.970
We've encoded our labels, and
we are almost ready to move on

00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:09.460
to the questions.

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:14.700
Before we do that, we
need to split our data.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:17.060
So another important
concept in machine learning

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:19.530
is train/test split.

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:22.023
So we take the
majority of our data,

00:15:22.023 --> 00:15:23.690
and we'll use it for
training our model.

00:15:23.690 --> 00:15:25.580
In this case, we'll do 80%.

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:28.310
We set aside a smaller
portion of our data

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:30.110
to use for testing
so that we can

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:32.840
see how our model performs
on data that it has never

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:33.990
seen before.

00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:37.550
So in this case, we have 150,000
questions in our training set

00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:41.070
and 37,000 in our test set.

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:50.000
So we will now split our labels
into training and test sets.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:54.050
And we'll say test tags
equals tags encoded.

00:15:57.640 --> 00:15:58.460
We'll run that.

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:00.560
So we've got our
labels ready to go.

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:03.730
And the next thing
we want to do--

00:16:03.730 --> 00:16:05.600
our label data is ready to go.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:08.080
Now, we need to encode
our question data

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:11.140
into bag of words matrices.

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:13.105
So I've written a
class to do this.

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:18.952
I'll paste that in here.

00:16:18.952 --> 00:16:20.910
It looks like a lot going
on, but I'll explain.

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:23.880
So what's happening here
is we're using this Keras

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:25.200
Tokenizer utility.

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:27.210
So luckily, we don't
have to do all that code

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:31.320
of converting our freeform
text to bag of words by hand.

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:33.450
Keras has this utility
that'll do it for us.

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:36.610
All we do is pass it
our vocabulary size.

00:16:36.610 --> 00:16:39.600
So in the super simple
example that I showed before,

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:41.170
we had a vocabulary size of 10.

00:16:41.170 --> 00:16:43.770
So that will take the top
10 words from our data set.

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:46.140
Since this data
set is way bigger,

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:49.387
I chose a vocab size of 400,
which you'll see in a moment.

00:16:49.387 --> 00:16:50.970
So you want to choose
something that's

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:54.300
not so small that it only
identifies common words

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:55.750
across all of your questions.

00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:57.125
But you also don't
want to choose

00:16:57.125 --> 00:16:59.670
something that's too big
that your bag of words

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:02.350
arrays become all 1s.

00:17:02.350 --> 00:17:03.780
So we'll run this.

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:06.300
And now, we're actually
going to use that class.

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:15.680
So we will import it, and
we will split our questions

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:18.920
into train and test sets.

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:19.710
So I'll do that.

00:17:23.069 --> 00:17:24.932
Right now, there's
still strings.

00:17:24.932 --> 00:17:26.099
We haven't encoded them yet.

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:36.110
So the next step is to
instantiate a vocab size

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:39.310
variable, which is
going to be 400.

00:17:39.310 --> 00:17:41.400
And now, we will actually
tokenize that text.

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:44.040
So we're going to create a
variable called processor

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:48.180
and instantiate that text
pre-process or class,

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:51.580
passing it our vocab size.

00:17:51.580 --> 00:17:56.320
And now we can call
processor.create_tokenizer

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:57.720
and pass it our train questions.

00:17:57.720 --> 00:18:00.230
That's this method, right here.

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:03.460
And then finally, the last
part is actually creating

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:05.560
those bag of words matrices.

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:10.330
So this will be equal to
processor.transform_text,

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:13.340
that method right there.

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:15.730
And we'll pass that
our train questions.

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:22.830
And then the test
set will be the same

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:26.160
with our test questions,
which we defined up here.

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:27.480
So I'm going to run that.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:30.147
It's going to take a bit of time
to run, because what it's doing

00:18:30.147 --> 00:18:33.480
is it's transforming all
180,000 of our questions

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:36.150
from text to bag of words.

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:41.000
So while that's running, we can
keep writing the other cells.

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:43.580
And they'll run when
this one completes.

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:46.700
So what I want to do
is print the length

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:48.240
of the first instance.

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:50.630
It should be 400.

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:54.860
And then we're just going to
log that bag of words matrix

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:57.820
so that you can see it.

00:18:57.820 --> 00:19:01.210
And now, we get to
building our model.

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:03.790
We'll come back to this
when it finishes running.

00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:05.590
So the next thing
that we want to do

00:19:05.590 --> 00:19:08.950
is save that tokenizer
that we created,

00:19:08.950 --> 00:19:13.590
because we're going to need
it when we deploy our model.

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:15.660
We're using a Python
utility called

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:20.020
Pickle to save that
tokenizer object to a file.

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:22.660
So now, it's time
to create our model.

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:24.540
We've transformed our data.

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:26.100
We've saved our tokenizer.

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:27.810
Now, we actually want
to write the code

00:19:27.810 --> 00:19:30.600
to our model using Keras.

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:33.000
I'm going to wrap
this in a method.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:36.600
We'll call it create_model,
and it will take our vocabulary

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:40.920
size and our number of tags.

00:19:40.920 --> 00:19:45.540
And we're going to use the
Keras sequential model API to do

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:48.060
that, which I imported above.

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:50.387
This is my favorite API
for building models,

00:19:50.387 --> 00:19:52.470
because it essentially
lets you define your models

00:19:52.470 --> 00:19:54.280
as a stack of layers.

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:56.280
So you'll see that the
code we're about to write

00:19:56.280 --> 00:19:58.710
will correspond really
nicely to that model diagram

00:19:58.710 --> 00:19:59.940
that I just showed.

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:01.930
So it looks like this
cell finished running.

00:20:01.930 --> 00:20:05.760
So we can see that this is what
the first bag of words input

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:08.130
looks like for our first
question, this array of 1s

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:10.760
and 0s.

00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:13.380
So let's continue
building our model.

00:20:13.380 --> 00:20:16.370
So the first layer
to our model is

00:20:16.370 --> 00:20:18.290
going to be a dense,
fully connected layer.

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:21.140
That just means that every
neuron in the input layer

00:20:21.140 --> 00:20:24.260
is connected to every
neuron in the output layer.

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:27.260
We need to tell our
first layer what

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:30.110
the input shape is going to be.

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:32.930
In this case, it'll be the
input of our vocab size, which

00:20:32.930 --> 00:20:34.850
is 400.

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:38.120
And then finally, we
need to tell Keras

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:40.850
how to compute the
output of this layer.

00:20:40.850 --> 00:20:42.920
The great thing
about Keras is we

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:46.490
don't need to know exactly how
this activation function works

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:47.540
under the hood.

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:50.340
We just need to know the correct
activation function to use.

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:53.400
So in this case, relu
is what we'll be using.

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:58.610
We'll add one more hidden layer
also with the relu activation

00:20:58.610 --> 00:20:59.247
function.

00:20:59.247 --> 00:21:01.580
We don't need input shape
here, because it will infer it

00:21:01.580 --> 00:21:04.810
from the previous layer.

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:07.330
So far, these are
our hidden layers.

00:21:07.330 --> 00:21:10.330
So we don't care too much about
the output of those layers.

00:21:10.330 --> 00:21:14.350
But what we do care about is
the output of our final layer.

00:21:14.350 --> 00:21:17.050
And that layer is going
to have five neurons.

00:21:17.050 --> 00:21:19.330
It'll be a five-element
array, since we

00:21:19.330 --> 00:21:21.370
have five tags in our data set.

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:26.290
And the activation function
here will be sigmoid.

00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:28.890
So that's really all
the code for our model.

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:32.700
Just four lines of code, we've
defined our model in Keras.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:35.850
We need to run model.compile
so that we can actually

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:38.290
train and evaluate this model.

00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:41.490
And to do this, we need to
tell it a couple of things.

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:44.040
So we need to tell
it our loss function.

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:47.470
This is how Keras will compute
the error of our model.

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:49.350
So every time it
runs training, it

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:51.820
will use this
function to say, OK,

00:21:51.820 --> 00:21:54.510
what was the error between
what the model predicted

00:21:54.510 --> 00:21:55.590
and the ground truth--

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:57.270
what it should have predicted?

00:21:57.270 --> 00:21:58.770
Again, we don't
need to know exactly

00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:02.170
how this particular loss
function works under the hood.

00:22:02.170 --> 00:22:05.850
This is just the best one to
use for this type of model.

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:09.420
I also need to tell
it my optimizer.

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:12.060
The optimizer is
how the model will

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:16.560
update the weights after it
goes through a set of data.

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:19.980
And then finally,
I want to tell it

00:22:19.980 --> 00:22:22.890
how to evaluate my
model as its training.

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:25.830
And we're going to use
accuracy as a metric here.

00:22:25.830 --> 00:22:29.190
So I will return my
model, and now we'll

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:31.935
actually create our model.

00:22:31.935 --> 00:22:33.060
So we'll call create_model.

00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:36.330
We'll pass it our vocab
size and our num tags.

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:38.790
And then we can use this
model.summary method

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:42.420
to see what our model
looks like, layer by layer.

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:43.590
So we can run that.

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:45.700
We've got our model ready to go.

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:47.380
We have not trained it yet.

00:22:47.380 --> 00:22:50.220
So the next thing we'll do
is we'll train our model.

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:55.060
And we can do that with one
method call, just model.fit.

00:22:55.060 --> 00:22:56.310
We pass it a couple of things.

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:57.900
We pass it our bag
of words array.

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:01.080
That's our input, our features.

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:02.490
We pass it our labels.

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:05.100
Those are the tags,
which we've encoded.

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:09.360
We need to tell it how many
epochs to run training for.

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:12.510
What this means is that this is
how many times our model will

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:15.060
iterate over our
entire data set.

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:19.330
So we'll go through our
entire data set three times.

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:23.950
Batch size is how many elements
our model will look at one time

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:25.750
before it updates the weights.

00:23:25.750 --> 00:23:28.220
In this case, we'll use 128.

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:31.000
And then I'm going to
pass an optional parameter

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:33.100
called validation
split, which will

00:23:33.100 --> 00:23:36.430
take 10% of our training
data and evaluate it

00:23:36.430 --> 00:23:38.740
as our model is training.

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:40.840
So we're going to
run training, and it

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:43.720
should train pretty fast.

00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:47.500
And what we ideally see
here is that our validation

00:23:47.500 --> 00:23:50.943
and our training
loss is decreasing.

00:23:50.943 --> 00:23:51.860
So that's a good sign.

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:55.720
We've got our validation and
training loss decreasing.

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:59.140
If your validation loss is
increasing as your training

00:23:59.140 --> 00:24:01.180
loss is decreasing,
it might be a sign

00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:04.850
that your model is learning
the training data too closely.

00:24:04.850 --> 00:24:09.940
We can see that our accuracy
is 96%, which is pretty good.

00:24:09.940 --> 00:24:12.880
So next step is
evaluating our model.

00:24:12.880 --> 00:24:15.760
So that 20% of the
questions that we set aside,

00:24:15.760 --> 00:24:18.890
we're now going to see how our
model performs on that data.

00:24:18.890 --> 00:24:21.340
So we call model.evalute.

00:24:21.340 --> 00:24:26.200
We pass it our test bag
of words, our test tags,

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:30.030
and we tell it our batch size.

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:32.880
And our evaluation accuracy
is very close to our training

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:35.268
accuracy, which is a good sign.

00:24:35.268 --> 00:24:36.810
And then the last
thing we want to do

00:24:36.810 --> 00:24:40.700
is save our model to a file.

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:44.790
Keras uses the H5 file format.

00:24:44.790 --> 00:24:47.060
So we'll save our model.

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:49.940
And now, we're going
to test it out locally.

00:24:49.940 --> 00:24:55.700
So we have a custom prediction
class that I've written,

00:24:55.700 --> 00:25:00.570
and all this is going to
do is pre-process our data.

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:03.720
So it'll instantiate our
SavedModel from the file.

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:05.810
It'll instantiate our tokenizer.

00:25:05.810 --> 00:25:09.940
It'll take the question
as text, transform it,

00:25:09.940 --> 00:25:13.680
and then return a prediction--
that sigmoid probability array.

00:25:13.680 --> 00:25:17.650
So let's save some
questions to predict.

00:25:17.650 --> 00:25:20.920
This is just an array
of two sample questions.

00:25:20.920 --> 00:25:24.130
And then we will make a
prediction on our local model.

00:25:27.260 --> 00:25:28.760
So this should return.

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:29.260
Cool.

00:25:29.260 --> 00:25:30.885
So for the first
question, it predicted

00:25:30.885 --> 00:25:32.450
Keras, which is accurate.

00:25:32.450 --> 00:25:35.780
The second one predicted Pandas.

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:37.670
So we've got our
model working locally.

00:25:37.670 --> 00:25:39.650
Accuracy is 96%.

00:25:39.650 --> 00:25:41.840
Next thing we want to do
is package and deploy it

00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:43.880
to AI Platform.

00:25:43.880 --> 00:25:45.455
So I've written some
code to do this.

00:25:48.850 --> 00:25:50.400
And one of the
features that we're

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:53.190
going to make use of that's
new to Cloud AI Platform

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:55.320
is Custom Code,
which lets us write

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:59.310
custom server-side Python code
that's run at prediction time.

00:25:59.310 --> 00:26:01.050
Now, this is really
useful, because it'll

00:26:01.050 --> 00:26:03.430
let us keep our
client super simple.

00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:06.477
So our client will just
pass the text to our model.

00:26:06.477 --> 00:26:09.060
We don't want to have to do any
transformations on the client.

00:26:09.060 --> 00:26:10.750
We'll pass our
text to the model.

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:13.130
And on the server, we'll
transform that text

00:26:13.130 --> 00:26:17.790
to bag of words and then
return the correct tags.

00:26:17.790 --> 00:26:21.550
So to do this, we'll need to
copy some files to Google Cloud

00:26:21.550 --> 00:26:26.090
Storage so that Cloud
AI Platform can find

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:28.850
our model and our tokenizer.

00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:31.400
I'm going to use the
Google Cloud CLI G

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:35.780
Cloud to set my current
project to the one I've

00:26:35.780 --> 00:26:39.130
configured for this demo.

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:42.145
And finally, we want to
deploy this to AI Platform.

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:48.070
So here's the deploy command
that we're going to run.

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:50.950
We're going to set our minimum
nodes to 1, so that our model

00:26:50.950 --> 00:26:52.840
doesn't scale to 0.

00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:57.910
And I'm going to create a
new version called io19.

00:26:57.910 --> 00:27:00.910
And we are going to deploy that.

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:02.950
That'll take a couple
of minutes to run.

00:27:02.950 --> 00:27:07.460
But if I look over here in the
Cloud AI Platform model UI,

00:27:07.460 --> 00:27:09.400
we can see that my
model is deploying,

00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:11.230
which is pretty cool.

00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:14.260
So while that's deploying,
let's go back to the slides.

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:22.130
So we built something
that looks pretty good.

00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:24.550
We've written some code
to pre-process our data.

00:27:24.550 --> 00:27:27.490
We've trained a model
with pretty high accuracy,

00:27:27.490 --> 00:27:29.230
but can we do better?

00:27:29.230 --> 00:27:31.790
Right now, our model is
pretty much a black box.

00:27:31.790 --> 00:27:33.670
We don't know how it's
making predictions.

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:35.480
We know that the
predictions are accurate,

00:27:35.480 --> 00:27:39.177
but we don't know what's making
it make those conclusions.

00:27:39.177 --> 00:27:40.760
So as Sundar mentioned
in the keynote,

00:27:40.760 --> 00:27:43.372
we want to make sure that
our model is using data

00:27:43.372 --> 00:27:44.830
to make predictions
in a way that's

00:27:44.830 --> 00:27:47.980
fair to all of our model users.

00:27:47.980 --> 00:27:52.210
To do that, we need to
uncover the black box.

00:27:52.210 --> 00:27:54.670
And as model builders,
we need to remember

00:27:54.670 --> 00:27:57.460
that we're responsible for
the predictions generated

00:27:57.460 --> 00:27:58.390
by our model.

00:27:58.390 --> 00:28:00.610
This may sound really
obvious, but we

00:28:00.610 --> 00:28:04.060
need to take steps to
avoid bias by making sure

00:28:04.060 --> 00:28:06.550
that our training
data is representative

00:28:06.550 --> 00:28:10.320
of the people that
are using our model.

00:28:10.320 --> 00:28:13.320
Luckily, there's a lot of great
tools to help you do this.

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:16.470
I'm going to look at one
open source tool called SHAP.

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:20.010
And what SHAP will let us
do is interpret the output

00:28:20.010 --> 00:28:21.490
of any machine learning model.

00:28:21.490 --> 00:28:25.050
So we'll use it here
for our tf.keras model,

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:28.340
but we could use it
for any type of model.

00:28:28.340 --> 00:28:30.590
The way SHAP works
is we'll create

00:28:30.590 --> 00:28:33.500
what's called an explainer
object, passing in our model

00:28:33.500 --> 00:28:36.620
and a subset of
our training data.

00:28:36.620 --> 00:28:40.380
And then SHAP returns
these attribution values.

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:42.170
So what the
attribution values are

00:28:42.170 --> 00:28:45.440
is positive and negative
values indicating

00:28:45.440 --> 00:28:48.200
how much a particular
feature impacted

00:28:48.200 --> 00:28:49.740
our model's prediction.

00:28:49.740 --> 00:28:51.880
So let's say we had a
model with three features.

00:28:51.880 --> 00:28:54.960
We'd get back a three-element
array of attribution values.

00:28:54.960 --> 00:28:58.400
High positive values mean
that particular feature

00:28:58.400 --> 00:29:00.110
pushed our prediction up.

00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:03.420
Negative values would mean it
pushed our prediction down.

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:06.870
Well, how will this work
for our bag of words model?

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:09.320
So what I configured
SHAP to do was

00:29:09.320 --> 00:29:12.170
I had it treat that
400 element vocabulary

00:29:12.170 --> 00:29:14.600
array as 400 features.

00:29:14.600 --> 00:29:18.140
And so we get back 400
different attribution scores,

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:20.660
and we can take the highest
values from that and the lowest

00:29:20.660 --> 00:29:26.210
values to see which words had
the most impact on our model.

00:29:26.210 --> 00:29:27.850
So I wrote a little
code to do this.

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:30.225
I don't know if the colors
are showing up well, in there.

00:29:30.225 --> 00:29:33.010
But what this is is I
took the top five highest

00:29:33.010 --> 00:29:34.990
and lowest attribution
scores so that I

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:38.630
could see which words our model
was using to make predictions.

00:29:38.630 --> 00:29:40.990
So this particular
question is about Pandas,

00:29:40.990 --> 00:29:44.942
and it picked up on the words
column, dataframe, df, series,

00:29:44.942 --> 00:29:46.900
which is good, because
those are all words that

00:29:46.900 --> 00:29:49.980
are specific to Pandas.

00:29:49.980 --> 00:29:52.380
Words that contributed
least were for,

00:29:52.380 --> 00:29:55.110
each, and you, which
are fairly common

00:29:55.110 --> 00:29:57.910
words that you might find in
any Stack Overflow question.

00:29:57.910 --> 00:30:02.560
So this is a good sign that
our model is working correctly.

00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:08.220
So let's go back to the demo
and take a look at SHAP.

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:10.470
So it looks like our
model finished deploying.

00:30:10.470 --> 00:30:15.160
I'm going to go ahead and set
that as the default version.

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:18.220
And before we get into SHAP, we
will generate some predictions

00:30:18.220 --> 00:30:20.210
on our deployed model.

00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:24.490
So I'm going to write a file
with some test predictions.

00:30:24.490 --> 00:30:27.420
And now, we're going
to use the Google Cloud

00:30:27.420 --> 00:30:31.000
CLI to get some predictions
on our trained model.

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:34.570
So we call gcloud
ai-platform predict.

00:30:34.570 --> 00:30:39.010
We pass it our model name,
which is stacko_model.

00:30:39.010 --> 00:30:44.480
And we pass it our
text instances,

00:30:44.480 --> 00:30:50.640
which is this predictions.txt
file that we just saved.

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:52.060
And we will run that.

00:30:55.200 --> 00:30:59.630
And now, we are going to print
our deployed model predictions.

00:31:05.250 --> 00:31:06.650
So it looks good.

00:31:06.650 --> 00:31:09.230
We're calling our model that's
deployed on AI Platform,

00:31:09.230 --> 00:31:11.780
and it has predicted
Keras successfully

00:31:11.780 --> 00:31:15.920
for the first question and
Pandas for the second question.

00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:21.310
So now, let's use SHAP
to explain our model.

00:31:21.310 --> 00:31:24.850
So SHAP does not come
pre-installed in Colab,

00:31:24.850 --> 00:31:26.890
so we will install it via PIP.

00:31:26.890 --> 00:31:29.260
And then we will also
install this module

00:31:29.260 --> 00:31:32.350
called Colored, which I use
to color different words

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:34.000
of the text.

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:38.050
So once that's done installing,
we will import SHAP,

00:31:38.050 --> 00:31:41.918
and we can create our explainer.

00:31:41.918 --> 00:31:43.960
So we will create what's
called a deep explainer.

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:45.793
SHAP has many different
types of explainers.

00:31:45.793 --> 00:31:47.410
We're going to use
the deep explainer,

00:31:47.410 --> 00:31:51.940
and we're going to pass it a
subset of our training data.

00:31:51.940 --> 00:31:55.060
And then we will create our
array of attribution values.

00:31:55.060 --> 00:31:58.450
So we can call
explainer.shap_values.

00:31:58.450 --> 00:32:01.390
And we pass it a subset
of our test data.

00:32:01.390 --> 00:32:03.580
In this case, we'll
just take the first 25

00:32:03.580 --> 00:32:04.780
examples from our test set.

00:32:09.790 --> 00:32:11.753
So we've got our
attribution values.

00:32:11.753 --> 00:32:14.170
The next thing you want to do
is print them in a nice way.

00:32:14.170 --> 00:32:15.880
So first, to do
that, we're going

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:19.200
to take that tokenizer that
we created in Keras, which

00:32:19.200 --> 00:32:22.170
is a dictionary, and we're
going to convert it to an array

00:32:22.170 --> 00:32:25.680
so that we can take our
attribution values from SHAP

00:32:25.680 --> 00:32:28.180
and see which word
they correspond to.

00:32:28.180 --> 00:32:32.970
So we'll convert our
tokenizer to a word list.

00:32:32.970 --> 00:32:37.560
And here, we can see the top
most frequently occurring words

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:40.775
that our model
vocabulary is using.

00:32:40.775 --> 00:32:42.150
And the first
thing we want to do

00:32:42.150 --> 00:32:47.340
is print a summary plot, which
is a method we have in SHAP,

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:49.230
to see a summary
of the words that

00:32:49.230 --> 00:32:52.840
are most impactful on
our model's predictions.

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:55.240
So we'll pass it
our SHAP values.

00:32:55.240 --> 00:32:57.930
We'll also need to pass it
our feature names, which

00:32:57.930 --> 00:33:02.070
is that word list
we just created,

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:05.550
along with our class names,
which goes all the way back

00:33:05.550 --> 00:33:08.670
to our tag encoder.

00:33:08.670 --> 00:33:11.260
So if I wrote that correctly,
we should get this nice plot,

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:12.100
here.

00:33:12.100 --> 00:33:16.542
And what this tells us is
the highest magnitude words

00:33:16.542 --> 00:33:17.750
that our model is looking at.

00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:19.420
So it looks like
dataframe was the word

00:33:19.420 --> 00:33:21.520
that our model used most
to make predictions,

00:33:21.520 --> 00:33:23.410
probably because there
was the most Pandas

00:33:23.410 --> 00:33:25.760
questions in our model.

00:33:25.760 --> 00:33:30.140
And we can see that dataframe
was most impactful for Pandas.

00:33:30.140 --> 00:33:33.290
It was also a signal word for
the other libraries, probably

00:33:33.290 --> 00:33:34.738
a negative signal word.

00:33:34.738 --> 00:33:37.280
And then we can see the other
words that were impactful here.

00:33:37.280 --> 00:33:41.780
So plot was most important
in Matplotlib predictions,

00:33:41.780 --> 00:33:43.330
which makes sense.

00:33:43.330 --> 00:33:46.310
And we can see all these
other words down the list.

00:33:46.310 --> 00:33:47.960
So that gives us a
nice at aggregate

00:33:47.960 --> 00:33:50.540
view of how our model
is making predictions

00:33:50.540 --> 00:33:52.430
for this particular data set.

00:33:52.430 --> 00:33:57.890
And now, if we want to
highlight individual words,

00:33:57.890 --> 00:34:00.110
I've written some code to
do that so that we can see,

00:34:00.110 --> 00:34:03.700
for a particular
question, which words

00:34:03.700 --> 00:34:05.990
are signaling predictions.

00:34:05.990 --> 00:34:09.820
So here's an example
of a Pandas question.

00:34:09.820 --> 00:34:12.280
The words column, a lot
of Pandas dataframes

00:34:12.280 --> 00:34:15.730
have columns that have
not a number of values.

00:34:15.730 --> 00:34:17.440
And then we see
common words like use

00:34:17.440 --> 00:34:20.460
and does are not being
used by our model

00:34:20.460 --> 00:34:21.460
to make the predictions.

00:34:21.460 --> 00:34:24.100
For TensorFlow,
we got it looking

00:34:24.100 --> 00:34:28.270
at words like session,
sesh, ops, tensor.

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:29.715
Here's a Keras example.

00:34:29.715 --> 00:34:31.340
It looks like the
most common words are

00:34:31.340 --> 00:34:35.980
LSTM, layers, dense, et cetera.

00:34:35.980 --> 00:34:37.750
So now, we can
see how we can use

00:34:37.750 --> 00:34:41.500
SHAP to make sure that our
model is behaving correctly.

00:34:41.500 --> 00:34:44.530
One way you might want to use
this in a fairness example

00:34:44.530 --> 00:34:47.239
is if you have a model that's
doing sentiment analysis,

00:34:47.239 --> 00:34:50.260
you want to make sure that it's
using the right characteristics

00:34:50.260 --> 00:34:54.280
to predict positive and negative
things in whatever type of text

00:34:54.280 --> 00:34:56.087
you're using.

00:34:56.087 --> 00:34:57.670
So if we could, go
back to the slides.

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:05.450
To wrap things up, we're
going to put this all together

00:35:05.450 --> 00:35:08.870
and get predictions from
our model in a web app.

00:35:08.870 --> 00:35:10.820
So it'd be really
useful if we could,

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:12.680
as we're typing a Stack
Overflow question,

00:35:12.680 --> 00:35:16.138
see which tags are
associated with it.

00:35:16.138 --> 00:35:18.180
So with the help of my
teammate Jennifer Person--

00:35:18.180 --> 00:35:20.100
you should follow
her on Twitter--

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:21.773
we built a Chrome extension.

00:35:21.773 --> 00:35:23.190
And what the Chrome
extension does

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:28.390
is it takes the HTML of our
Stack Overflow question.

00:35:28.390 --> 00:35:30.660
It passes it to
a Cloud Function,

00:35:30.660 --> 00:35:33.630
which I've written using the
Cloud Functions Python runtime.

00:35:33.630 --> 00:35:37.050
The function will use the
AI Platform API to make

00:35:37.050 --> 00:35:38.580
a prediction on our model.

00:35:38.580 --> 00:35:40.230
And then in our
function, we'll return

00:35:40.230 --> 00:35:43.500
just the name of
high confidence tags

00:35:43.500 --> 00:35:46.150
back to our Chrome extension.

00:35:46.150 --> 00:35:50.150
So let's see it in action.

00:35:50.150 --> 00:35:51.010
Whoops.

00:35:51.010 --> 00:35:53.280
Let's go back to the demo.

00:35:53.280 --> 00:35:54.570
Here we go.

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:58.440
So here is an example question
about Matplotlib in Pandas.

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:00.825
I have just loaded up my
Chrome extension, locally.

00:36:04.240 --> 00:36:06.510
Looks like it's not
working at the moment.

00:36:06.510 --> 00:36:07.320
Hold on one second.

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:10.535
Live demos, you never know.

00:36:10.535 --> 00:36:11.660
Let's try it out once more.

00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:17.730
Something's going on here.

00:36:17.730 --> 00:36:19.511
I'll try out the question draft.

00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:27.890
It's not working, but
